# NAVIGATION

Version 1.0

---

# Purpose

Navigation exists to support training.

It should never become the athlete's objective.

The athlete should always know:

- where they are;
- where they can go;
- how to return.

Navigation should require as little thought as possible.

---

# Core Principle

The shortest path between intention and action.

Every tap should feel obvious.

Every destination should have a clear purpose.

---

# Mental Model

CAS is not a collection of pages.

It is a training journey.

The navigation follows the athlete's workflow.

Not the application's internal architecture.

---

# Primary Navigation

The application uses five permanent sections.

Home

↓

Program

↓

Training

↓

Progress

↓

Settings

The navigation bar remains visible except during active training when full focus is required.

---

# Home

Purpose:

Answer one question:

"What should I do today?"

The Home screen is always the default entry point.

---

# Program

Purpose:

Allow the athlete to explore the current training plan.

The athlete may:

View upcoming sessions.

Review the current block.

Understand future progression.

This section is planning-oriented.

Not execution-oriented.

---

# Training

Purpose:

Execute today's workout.

During an active session this section becomes the operational center of the application.

No unnecessary navigation should interrupt training.

---

# Progress

Purpose:

Understand long-term evolution.

Contains:

Statistics

History

Records

Recommendations

This section supports reflection.

Not execution.

---

# Settings

Purpose:

Configure CAS.

This section should remain secondary.

The athlete should rarely need to visit it.

---

# Navigation Hierarchy

Level 1

Bottom Navigation

↓

Level 2

Screen

↓

Level 3

Detail

↓

Level 4

Modal

The athlete should never become lost.

---

# Navigation Depth

Avoid deep navigation trees.

Recommended maximum:

Three levels.

If a fourth level becomes necessary,

the information architecture should be reconsidered.

---

# Screen Titles

Every screen has a clear title.

Titles describe destinations.

Not actions.

Good:

History

Settings

Program

Bad:

Click here

Let's train

Your awesome progress

---

# Back Navigation

Always use platform conventions.

The athlete should immediately recognize how to return.

Avoid custom back buttons.

---

# Modals

Use modals only for temporary tasks.

Examples:

Edit profile

Session notes

Exercise details

Filters

The athlete should always know how to dismiss the modal.

---

# Full Screen Experience

Only the active training session may temporarily hide navigation.

The athlete must remain focused.

The interface should minimize accidental exits.

---

# Session Protection

Leaving an active session should require confirmation.

Example:

Return later?

Your progress is already saved.

Primary action:

Continue Session

Secondary action:

Leave Session

Never silently abandon training.

---

# Deep Linking

Future versions should support direct navigation.

Examples:

Specific exercise

Training block

History entry

Statistics view

Notification destination

Navigation remains predictable.

---

# Search

Search should exist only where useful.

Possible locations:

Exercise library

History

Statistics

Do not add search simply because it is common.

---

# Tabs

Avoid nested tab bars.

One primary navigation system is sufficient.

Secondary categorization should use segmented controls when appropriate.

---

# Gestures

Support native gestures.

Examples:

Swipe back.

Scroll.

Pull to refresh.

Avoid hidden gesture-only interactions.

Every important action should remain discoverable.

---

# Orientation Changes

Navigation should remain coherent in portrait and landscape.

The athlete should never lose context after rotating the device.

---

# Empty Navigation

If a section contains no data,

explain why.

Provide the next logical action.

Never display empty pages.

---

# Notifications

Notifications should always open the relevant destination.

Examples:

Today's workout

↓

Today's Mission

Recovery reminder

↓

Recovery screen

Session completed

↓

Session Summary

Avoid opening Home for every notification.

---

# Error Recovery

If navigation fails,

explain clearly.

Offer recovery.

Never leave the athlete on a broken screen.

---

# Accessibility

Support:

VoiceOver

Dynamic Type

Large touch targets

Logical focus order

Keyboard navigation (future)

Navigation should never rely solely on gestures.

---

# Motion

Navigation transitions should feel natural.

Allowed:

Native transitions.

Subtle modal presentation.

Context-preserving animation.

Not allowed:

Complex page transitions.

Rotating screens.

3D effects.

Decorative motion.

Respect Reduced Motion.

---

# Color Usage

Navigation remains visually discreet.

The active destination is highlighted.

Inactive destinations remain secondary.

Avoid excessive accent colors.

Navigation should support content,

never dominate it.

---

# Consistency

Every destination should always appear in the same place.

Never move navigation items dynamically.

Predictability builds confidence.

---

# Future Expansion

Future modules may include:

Nutrition

Competition Preparation

Rehabilitation

Coach Portal

Team Management

These modules should integrate without breaking the primary navigation model.

---

# Success Criteria

The athlete should:

Always know where they are.

Always know what to do next.

Never feel lost.

Never search for common actions.

Reach any major destination within three taps.

---

# Do

Follow platform conventions.

Keep navigation shallow.

Protect active training.

Maintain consistency.

Support future expansion.

---

# Do Not

Hide important actions.

Multiply navigation systems.

Create deep menu hierarchies.

Interrupt training unnecessarily.

Move navigation items dynamically.

---

# Final Principle

Navigation should disappear.

The athlete remembers the training.

Not how they reached it.